<h1>Install metais</h1>

<p><code>metais</code> is one static binary with no dependencies. Each
release publishes an archive for each of these platforms, and a
<code>SHA256SUMS</code> file that lists the checksum of every archive:</p>

<ul>
<li>macOS on Apple silicon: <code>darwin_arm64</code></li>
<li>macOS on Intel: <code>darwin_amd64</code></li>
<li>Linux on x86-64: <code>linux_amd64</code></li>
<li>Linux on ARM64: <code>linux_arm64</code></li>
</ul>

<p>On Windows, <a href="#from-source">build it from source</a>.</p>

<h2 id="download">1. Download the latest release</h2>

<p>Open the latest release:
<a href="https://github.com/kottera/metais/releases/latest">github.com/kottera/metais/releases/latest</a>.
Its title is its version. Download two files from it: the archive for your
platform, <code>metais_&lt;version&gt;_&lt;os&gt;_&lt;arch&gt;.tar.gz</code>,
and <code>SHA256SUMS</code>.</p>

<p>With the <a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a>, this downloads
both for Linux on x86-64; change the pattern for another platform:</p>

<pre><code>gh release download --repo kottera/metais --pattern 'metais_*_linux_amd64.tar.gz' --pattern SHA256SUMS</code></pre>

<h2 id="verify">2. Verify the archive</h2>

<p>Check the archive against <code>SHA256SUMS</code> before you unpack it.
Run this in the directory that holds both files.</p>

<p>On Linux:</p>
<pre><code>sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS</code></pre>

<p>On macOS:</p>
<pre><code>shasum -a 256 --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS</code></pre>

<p>Each command prints the archive's name followed by <code>OK</code>. Any
other answer means the download is damaged or is not the release's: delete
it and do not use it.</p>

<p>The release's binaries are reproducible: building the release's tag with
the release builder in the repository gives the same bytes, so the
checksums can be checked independently of the download.</p>

<h2 id="unpack">3. Unpack it and put metais on your PATH</h2>

<p>The archive holds <code>metais</code> and its <code>LICENSE</code>,
<code>NOTICE</code> and <code>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt</code>.</p>

<pre><code>tar -xzf metais_*_linux_amd64.tar.gz metais
sudo install metais /usr/local/bin/</code></pre>

<p>Any directory on your <code>PATH</code> will do, such as
<code>~/.local/bin</code> without <code>sudo</code>. On macOS, if Gatekeeper
refuses a binary downloaded with a browser, remove its quarantine attribute
after verifying it: <code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine metais</code>.</p>

<h2 id="check">4. Check it runs</h2>

<pre><code>metais version
metais help</code></pre>

<p><code>metais version</code> prints the tool's version and the
specification versions it implements. <code>metais help</code> is the
tool's own reference: every command, flag and refusal is in it, and
<code>metais help &lt;command&gt;</code> prints one command's.</p>

<p>Next: <a href="/getting-started/">getting started</a>.</p>

<h2 id="from-source">Building from source</h2>

<p>With Go installed, clone the repository at the release's tag and build
it. Its <code>go.mod</code> names the exact Go toolchain, which Go fetches
by itself.</p>

<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/kottera/metais.git
cd metais
git checkout "$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match 'v*')"
CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath ./cmd/metais</code></pre>

<p>A binary built this way is not the release's: <code>metais version</code>
reports <code>0.0.0-devel</code>.</p>
